<Page
	title="About"
	description="Harry Munroe: a translator between what's being invented and the people who have to run things."
>
	<div class="prose">
		<h2>Who I am</h2>
		<p>
			I'm from New England, with a Nordic thread running through my family and my interests. They
			are two high-latitude, forested, maritime places wrestling with the same questions about
			energy, resilience, and how to keep people connected over long distances in sparsely
			settled places.
		</p>
		<p>
			I love collecting ideas the way some people collect old issues of National Geographic. I
			sometimes feel like I'm in a state of perpetual preparation: one more book or article and
			<em>then</em> I'll be able to act. But I want to break that cycle and contribute my own
			ideas back. I won't get feedback on them, or find people to build with, unless I'm writing
			as much as I'm consuming. This site is my attempt to move from collecting to contributing.
		</p>

		<h2>Who I write for</h2>
		<p>
			I write for people with real responsibility who privately suspect they're still beginners.
			The director whose domain expertise got her the seat, but who now fields questions about AI
			she can't confidently answer. The official who signs off on infrastructure he won't live to
			see finished. If you've ever sat in a meeting nodding at a term you couldn't define, this
			site is for you, and I'd rather you nod with confidence next time.
		</p>

		<h2>What I actually do</h2>
		<p>
			My work has always been translation. As a healthcare IT consultant, I helped people adopt
			systems they didn't ask for. In technology sales and enablement, I made complex things easy
			to connect with. Now I work where emerging technologies like AI, quantum computing, and
			advanced energy systems meet governments and the institutions that keep societies
			running. I take
			what's being invented and make it usable by the people who have to decide things.
		</p>
		<p>
			The through-line I care about most: energy and compute are the two great levers of this
			century, and the hardest work ahead is designing the operating system of institutions,
			standards, and decisions that makes these systems run.
		</p>
		<p>
			This year IBM is loaning me out to the National Defense University, where I'll study
			industrial mobilization, supply chains, and how institutions hold up under strain. I'll
			have the least military experience in most of those rooms, which is most of why I'm going.
		</p>

		<h2>Reach me</h2>
		<p>
			The best way is email: <a href="mailto:harry@harrymunroe.com">harry@harrymunroe.com</a>. I
			read everything and answer most of it. I'm also on
			<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/harrymunroe/">LinkedIn</a>.
		</p>

		<p class="epistemic">
			The postings on this site are my own and don't necessarily represent the positions,
			strategies, or opinions of IBM, the National Defense University, or the U.S. government.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
